      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the positive value of \\( t \\) such that \\( |6 + ti| = 10 \\), we need to use the formula for the magnitude of a complex number. The magnitude of a complex number \\( a + bi \\) is given by \\( \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2} \\).\n\nIn this problem, the complex number is \\( 6 + ti \\), so \\( a = 6 \\) and \\( b = t \\). The magnitude is given by:\n\\[\n|6 + ti| = \\sqrt{6^2 + t^2} = \\sqrt{36 + t^2}\n\\]\nWe are given that this magnitude is equal to 10, so we can set up the equation:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{36 + t^2} = 10\n\\]\nTo eliminate the square root, we square both sides of the equation:\n\\[\n36 + t^2 = 100\n\\]\nNext, we isolate \\( t^2 \\) by subtracting 36 from both sides:\n\\[\nt^2 = 64\n\\]\nTo solve for \\( t \\), we take the square root of both sides:\n\\[\nt = \\pm 8\n\\]\nSince we are asked for the positive value of \\( t \\), we have:\n\\[\nt = 8\n\\]\nThus, the positive value of \\( t \\) is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).",
