      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve for the positive value of \\( t \\) such that \\( |6 + ti| = 10 \\), we start by recalling the definition of the magnitude (or modulus) of a complex number. For a complex number \\( a + bi \\), the magnitude is given by:\n\n\\[\n|a + bi| = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}\n\\]\n\nIn this problem, the complex number is \\( 6 + ti \\). Therefore, its magnitude is:\n\n\\[\n|6 + ti| = \\sqrt{6^2 + t^2}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that this magnitude is equal to 10:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{6^2 + t^2} = 10\n\\]\n\nNext, we square both sides of the equation to eliminate the square root:\n\n\\[\n6^2 + t^2 = 10^2\n\\]\n\nSimplify the squares:\n\n\\[\n36 + t^2 = 100\n\\]\n\nSubtract 36 from both sides to isolate \\( t^2 \\):\n\n\\[\nt^2 = 100 - 36\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt^2 = 64\n\\]\n\nTo find \\( t \\), take the square root of both sides:\n\n\\[\nt = \\sqrt{64}\n\\]\n\nSince we are asked for the positive value of \\( t \\):\n\n\\[\nt = 8\n\\]\n\nThus, the positive value of \\( t \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{8}\n\\]",
